## Why This Is Asked
This question targets advanced serverless scheduling with CKNE-aware fairness, addressing a critical gap in multi-tenant FaaS resource management.

## Key Concepts
- **CKNE-to-resource mapping**: Weighted allocation formulas ensuring proportional resource distribution
- **Rebalancing mechanics**: Minute-level redistribution with weighted fair queueing and preemption budgets
- **SLA preservation**: Baseline guarantees through fallback mechanisms and priority queuing
- **Essential telemetry**: Per-tenant latency metrics, resource utilization patterns, and tail latency analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
function calculateQuotas(ckne, baseConcurrency, baseMemory, baseBudget) {
  const weight = 0.4 + 0.6 * ckne; // Primary resource weight
  const coldWeight = 0.3 + 0.7 * ckne; // Cold-start budget weight

  return {
    concurrency: Math.round(baseConcurrency * weight),
    memory: Math.round(baseMemory * weight),
    coldStartBudget: Math.round(baseBudget * coldWeight)
  };
}

// Rebalancing logic
function rebalanceInvocations(pendingInvocations) {
  const lowCKNE = pendingInvocations.filter(i => i.ckne < 0.5);
  const highCKNE = pendingInvocations.filter(i => i.ckne > 0.7);

  return redistributeWithFairness(lowCKNE, highCKNE);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses the ability to design a CKNE-aware ingestion control loop that dynamically adapts per-tenant throughput while ensuring SLA guarantees and system stability under CKNE shifts.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-to-priority mapping: P = 1 + ckne (higher CKNE → higher priority)
- CKNE-to-batch window mapping: W = baseW * (1 - ckne/2) (higher CKNE → smaller batches, faster processing)
- Exponential backoff: delay = baseDelay * 2^retryCount for retries
- Minute-by-minute scheduler: reweights quota allocation each cycle based on current CKNE
- SLA preservation: baseline minimum quota guaranteed even for ckne=0 tenants

## Code Example

```javascript
function quotaForTenant(ckne, baseBatchWindowMs, basePriority) {
  const priority = basePriority + ckne;
  const batchWindow = baseBatchWindowMs * (1 - ckne / 2);
  return { priority, batchWindow };
}

// Example: tenant A with ckne=0.65
quotaForTenant(0.65, 60000, 1); 
// → { priority: 1.65, batchWindow: 31500ms }
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to translate CKNE (Customer Key Need Engagement) into concrete cache behavior, tiering strategies, and fairness mechanisms under SLA constraints, while implementing a time-based rebalancing loop. It combines practical edge cache architecture with state management and real-time optimization.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-to-cache allocation mapping
- Tier promotion/demotion logic
- Minute-by-minute rebalancing with SLA guarantees
- Weighted eviction policy integration (LRU with CKNE weights)

## Code Example

```javascript
function adjustQuotas(tenants, ckneMap, totalSlots) {
  // Compute per-tenant target slots based on CKNE
  const weights = tenants.map(t => Math.max(0.01, ckneMap[t] ?? 0));
  const weightSum = weights.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);

  // Allocate baseline + CKNE-proportional slots
  const baselineSlots = Math.floor(totalSlots * 0.3 / tenants.length);
  const dynamicSlots = totalSlots * 0.7;

  return tenants.map((tenant, i) => ({
    tenant,
    totalSlots: baselineSlots + Math.floor(dynamicSlots * weights[i] / weightSum),
    hotTTL: 60 * (1 + ckneMap[tenant]), // 1-2 minutes based on CKNE
    warmTTL: 300 * (1 + ckneMap[tenant] * 2), // 5-15 minutes
    coldTTL: 1800 * (1 + ckneMap[tenant] * 3) // 30-90 minutes
  }));
}
```

